-- title:
--   The hypersurface algebra $\mathbb{C}[p,s,u,F,J]/(H)$ is not a polynomial ring in four variables
-- statement:
--   Let $P=\mathbb{C}[p,s,u,F,J]$ be the polynomial ring over the complex numbers in five independent variables $p,s,u,F,J$. Put
--
--   $$
--   x=s^2+u^3+p^2F,\qquad H=x^2F-(1+2sx)J-p^2J^2-pu\in P,
--   $$
--
--   and let $A=P/(H)$ be the quotient of $P$ by the principal ideal generated by $H$, regarded as a $\mathbb{C}$-algebra.
--
--   **Theorem.** There is no isomorphism of $\mathbb{C}$-algebras between $A$ and the polynomial ring in four variables:
--
--   $$
--   A=\mathbb{C}[p,s,u,F,J]/(H)\;\not\cong\;\mathbb{C}[X_1,X_2,X_3,X_4].
--   $$
--
--   Equivalently, the affine hypersurface $\operatorname{Spec}A=\{H=0\}\subset\mathbb{A}^5_{\mathbb{C}}$ is not isomorphic over $\mathbb{C}$ to affine four-space $\mathbb{A}^4_{\mathbb{C}}$.
--
--   The same algebra $A$ is a four-dimensional integral domain whose cylinder $A[t]$ is isomorphic to $\mathbb{C}[X_1,\dots,X_5]$. This theorem is the non-polynomiality half of that picture: it shows that the extra variable $t$ cannot be cancelled, so $A$ is an explicit failure of cancellation for complex affine four-space.
--
--   **Formalization Note** $P$ is `MvPolynomial (Fin 5) ℂ`, whose variables $X_0,\dots,X_4$ are named $p,s,u,F,J$; $A$ is the quotient by `Ideal.span {H}`; and the statement is `¬ Nonempty (A ≃ₐ[ℂ] MvPolynomial (Fin 4) ℂ)`, so it rules out every $\mathbb{C}$-algebra isomorphism, with no condition on how it treats the variables.
